Answer: The transformation you describe by examples is not a rotation, in fact it does not preserve norms, it is the projection on the real axis. If instead you want a $45°$-counterclockwise rotation, apply the rotation matrix
$$\left(
\begin{array}{cc}
\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\
\sin\theta&\cos\theta
\end{array}
\right)$$ 
 with $\theta=\frac{\pi}{4}$ 
