{"input_text": "Question: How can I calculate the integral $I=\int_0^1\frac{\ln(1+x)}{1+x^2}\,dx$ by substituting $t=\frac{1-x}{1+x}$. 

Answer: Another way. Setting $x= \tan(t)$, we get that
\begin{align}
I & = \int_0^{\pi/4} \ln(1+\tan(t))dt = \int_0^{\pi/4} \ln \left(\sqrt2 \cos(\pi/4-t)\right) dt - \int_0^{\pi/4} \ln(\cos(t)) dt\\
& = \int_0^{\pi/4} \ln(\sqrt2) dt + \underbrace{\int_0^{\pi/4}\ln \left(\cos(\pi/4-t)\right) dt}_{\pi/4-t \to t \text{ gives }\int_0^{\pi/4} \ln(\cos(t)) dt} - \int_0^{\pi/4} \ln(\cos(t)) dt\\
& = \dfrac{\pi \ln(2)}8
\end{align} 
