{"input_text": "Question: How can you calculate the integral $$\int_a^b\frac{\exp(kx)}{x}\,\mathrm dx$$for any $k>0$? 

Answer: The integral cannot be expressed by elementary functions, instead what one obtain is $$\int_{a}^{b}\frac{e^{kx}}{x}dx=Ei(bk)-Ei(ak)$$ that is, when the integral is defined. So, if I want to calculate the integral $\int_{1}^{2}\frac{e^{2x}}{x}dx$, I use the this result to obtain $$\int_{1}^{2}\frac{e^{2x}}{x}dx=Ei(4)-Ei(2) \approx 14.6766$$ Now, if you would want to approximate the integral then you could try to express the function as a serie. In our case that would be $$\int_{1}^{2}\sum_{n=-1}^{∞}\frac{2^{n+1}x^{n}}{(1+n)!}dx $$ 
